<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Mahdid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1159 CE – 1174 CE · capital Zabid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A puritanical reformist dynasty that seized Zabid in 1159 and ruled the Yemeni Tihama until the Ayyubid invasion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-mahdi">Ali ibn Mahdi</a>, a preacher from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>, built a following among the tribes of the coastal plain in the 1130s and 1140s, preaching strict morality and condemning the ruling elite. After years of raids he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> in 1159, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/najahid-dynasty">Najahid dynasty</a>, and died soon after.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His sons, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-nabi-ibn-ali-ibn-mahdi">Abd al-Nabi</a>, ran a militant state. Contemporary sources, mostly hostile, accuse them of treating other Muslims as unbelievers, punishing minor sins with death and destroying tombs. They raided widely across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zurayid-dynasty">Zurayid dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1174 the Ayyubid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turan-shah-ibn-ayyub">Turan-Shah</a>, sent by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-nabi-ibn-ali-ibn-mahdi">Abd al-Nabi</a>, absorbing the Mahdid lands into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ayyubid-yemen">Ayyubid Yemen</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mahdid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mahdid dynasty" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Tiwahi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemen_1160_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1159 CE – 1174 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zabid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Mahdi, Abd al-Nabi ibn Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (reformist movement)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mahdids, Banu Mahdi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1166&amp;focus=mahdid-dynasty" aria-label="Mahdid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5866 1649 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mahdid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6186 1905L6186 1905M6186 1905L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6186 1905h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6186 1905h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6186 1905h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zabid. <a href="/atlas?year=1166&amp;focus=mahdid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
